08:00:48 [INFO] root - Received request: {'train_sequences': ["Yuan dynasty\n1. The Yuan dynasty's bureaucracy was a blend of cultures, including Han Chinese, Khitans, Jurchens, Mongols, and Tibetan Buddhists.", 'Yuan dynasty\n2. The official terminology of the institutions may suggest a purely Chinese dynastic structure, but the bureaucracy was a mix of elements from different cultures.', 'Yuan dynasty\n3. The Chinese-style elements of the bureaucracy came from the native Tang, Song, Liao, and Jin dynasties.', "Yuan dynasty\n4. Chinese advisers such as Liu Bingzhong and Yao Shu had a significant influence on Kublai's early court.", "Yuan dynasty\n5. The central government administration was established within the first decade of Kublai's reign.", 'Yuan dynasty\n6. The government adopted a traditional Chinese tripartite division of authority among civil, military, and censorial offices.', 'Yuan dynasty\n7. The Central Secretariat (Zhongshu Sheng) managed civil affairs, while the Privy Council (Chinese: 樞密院) managed military affairs.', 'Yuan dynasty\n8. The Censorate conducted internal surveillance and inspection.', 'Yuan dynasty\n9. The actual functions of both central and local government institutions showed a major overlap between the civil and military jurisdictions due to the Mongol traditional reliance on military institutions and offices.', 'Yuan dynasty\n10. A civilian bureaucracy was created in China, with the Central Secretariat as the top institution responsible for most other governmental agencies.', 'Yuan dynasty\n11. At various times, another central government institution called the Department of State Affairs (Shangshu Sheng) that mainly dealt with finance was established, but was usually abandoned shortly afterwards.', "Yuan dynasty\n12. The Yuan dynasty's bureaucracy reflected the complex cultural landscape of the empire and the diverse influences of its rulers.", "Yuan dynasty\nThe system of bureaucracy created by Kublai Khan reflected various cultures in the empire, including that of the Han Chinese, Khitans, Jurchens, Mongols, and Tibetan Buddhists. While the official terminology of the institutions may indicate the government structure was almost purely that of native Chinese dynasties, the Yuan bureaucracy actually consisted of a mix of elements from different cultures. The Chinese-style elements of the bureaucracy mainly came from the native Tang, Song, as well as Khitan Liao and Jurchen Jin dynasties. Chinese advisers such as Liu Bingzhong and Yao Shu gave strong influence to Kublai's early court, and the central government administration was established within the first decade of Kublai's reign. This government adopted the traditional Chinese tripartite division of authority among civil, military, and censorial offices, including the Central Secretariat (Zhongshu Sheng) to manage civil affairs, the Privy Council (Chinese: 樞密院) to manage military affairs, and the Censorate to conduct internal surveillance and inspection. The actual functions of both central and local government institutions, however, showed a major overlap between the civil and military jurisdictions, due to the Mongol traditional reliance on military institutions and offices as the core of governance. Nevertheless, such a civilian bureaucracy, with the Central Secretariat as the top institution that was (directly or indirectly) responsible for most other governmental agencies (such as the traditional Chinese-style Six Ministries), was created in China. At various times another central government institution called the Department of State Affairs (Shangshu Sheng) that mainly dealt with finance was established (such as during the reign of Külüg Khan or Emperor Wuzong), but was usually abandoned shortly afterwards."], 'eval_questions': [{'title': 'Yuan dynasty', 'context': "The system of bureaucracy created by Kublai Khan reflected various cultures in the empire, including that of the Han Chinese, Khitans, Jurchens, Mongols, and Tibetan Buddhists. While the official terminology of the institutions may indicate the government structure was almost purely that of native Chinese dynasties, the Yuan bureaucracy actually consisted of a mix of elements from different cultures. The Chinese-style elements of the bureaucracy mainly came from the native Tang, Song, as well as Khitan Liao and Jurchen Jin dynasties. Chinese advisers such as Liu Bingzhong and Yao Shu gave strong influence to Kublai's early court, and the central government administration was established within the first decade of Kublai's reign. This government adopted the traditional Chinese tripartite division of authority among civil, military, and censorial offices, including the Central Secretariat (Zhongshu Sheng) to manage civil affairs, the Privy Council (Chinese: 樞密院) to manage military affairs, and the Censorate to conduct internal surveillance and inspection. The actual functions of both central and local government institutions, however, showed a major overlap between the civil and military jurisdictions, due to the Mongol traditional reliance on military institutions and offices as the core of governance. Nevertheless, such a civilian bureaucracy, with the Central Secretariat as the top institution that was (directly or indirectly) responsible for most other governmental agencies (such as the traditional Chinese-style Six Ministries), was created in China. At various times another central government institution called the Department of State Affairs (Shangshu Sheng) that mainly dealt with finance was established (such as during the reign of Külüg Khan or Emperor Wuzong), but was usually abandoned shortly afterwards.", 'question': "Topic: Yuan dynasty\nWhat cultures were part of Kublai's administration?", 'answer': 'Han Chinese, Khitans, Jurchens, Mongols, and Tibetan Buddhists'}, {'title': 'Yuan dynasty', 'context': "The system of bureaucracy created by Kublai Khan reflected various cultures in the empire, including that of the Han Chinese, Khitans, Jurchens, Mongols, and Tibetan Buddhists. While the official terminology of the institutions may indicate the government structure was almost purely that of native Chinese dynasties, the Yuan bureaucracy actually consisted of a mix of elements from different cultures. The Chinese-style elements of the bureaucracy mainly came from the native Tang, Song, as well as Khitan Liao and Jurchen Jin dynasties. Chinese advisers such as Liu Bingzhong and Yao Shu gave strong influence to Kublai's early court, and the central government administration was established within the first decade of Kublai's reign. This government adopted the traditional Chinese tripartite division of authority among civil, military, and censorial offices, including the Central Secretariat (Zhongshu Sheng) to manage civil affairs, the Privy Council (Chinese: 樞密院) to manage military affairs, and the Censorate to conduct internal surveillance and inspection. The actual functions of both central and local government institutions, however, showed a major overlap between the civil and military jurisdictions, due to the Mongol traditional reliance on military institutions and offices as the core of governance. Nevertheless, such a civilian bureaucracy, with the Central Secretariat as the top institution that was (directly or indirectly) responsible for most other governmental agencies (such as the traditional Chinese-style Six Ministries), was created in China. At various times another central government institution called the Department of State Affairs (Shangshu Sheng) that mainly dealt with finance was established (such as during the reign of Külüg Khan or Emperor Wuzong), but was usually abandoned shortly afterwards.", 'question': "Topic: Yuan dynasty\nWhat dynasties inspired the Chinese-like elements of Kublai's government?", 'answer': 'Tang, Song, as well as Khitan Liao and Jurchen Jin dynasties'}, {'title': 'Yuan dynasty', 'context': "The system of bureaucracy created by Kublai Khan 