08:28:40 [INFO] root - Received request: {'train_sequences': ['American Broadcasting Company\n1. American Broadcasting Company (ABC) Radio launched a special programming project for its FM stations in July 1968.', 'American Broadcasting Company\n2. The project was spearheaded by Allen Shaw, a former program manager at WCFL in Chicago.', 'American Broadcasting Company\n3. Shaw was approached by ABC Radio president Harold L. Neal to develop a format to compete with the new progressive rock and DJ-helmed stations.', 'American Broadcasting Company\n4. The new concept called "LOVE Radio" featured a limited selection of music genres.', "American Broadcasting Company\n5. LOVE Radio was launched on ABC's seven owned-and-operated FM stations in late November 1968.", 'American Broadcasting Company\n6. The concept replaced nearly all of the programming provided by these stations.', 'American Broadcasting Company\n7. Several affiliates, such as KXYZ, retained the majority of their content.', "American Broadcasting Company\n8. In August 1970, Shaw announced that ABC FM's music choice policy should be reviewed to allow listeners access to many styles of music.", 'American Broadcasting Company\nIn July 1968, ABC Radio launched a special programming project for its FM stations, which was spearheaded by Allen Shaw, a former program manager at WCFL in Chicago who was approached by ABC Radio president Harold L. Neal to develop a format to compete with the new progressive rock and DJ-helmed stations. The new concept called "LOVE Radio", which featured a limited selection of music genres, was launched on ABC\'s seven owned-and-operated FM stations in late November 1968; the concept replaced nearly all of the programming provided by these stations; however, several affiliates (such as KXYZ) retained the majority of their content. In August 1970, Shaw announced that ABC FM\'s music choice policy should be reviewed to allow listeners access to many styles of music.'], 'eval_questions': [{'title': 'American Broadcasting Company', 'context': 'In July 1968, ABC Radio launched a special programming project for its FM stations, which was spearheaded by Allen Shaw, a former program manager at WCFL in Chicago who was approached by ABC Radio president Harold L. Neal to develop a format to compete with the new progressive rock and DJ-helmed stations. The new concept called "LOVE Radio", which featured a limited selection of music genres, was launched on ABC\'s seven owned-and-operated FM stations in late November 1968; the concept replaced nearly all of the programming provided by these stations; however, several affiliates (such as KXYZ) retained the majority of their content. In August 1970, Shaw announced that ABC FM\'s music choice policy should be reviewed to allow listeners access to many styles of music.', 'question': "Topic: American Broadcasting Company\nWho lead the 1968 special programming for ABC Radio's FM stations?", 'answer': 'Allen Shaw'}, {'title': 'American Broadcasting Company', 'context': 'In July 1968, ABC Radio launched a special programming project for its FM stations, which was spearheaded by Allen Shaw, a former program manager at WCFL in Chicago who was approached by ABC Radio president Harold L. Neal to develop a format to compete with the new progressive rock and DJ-helmed stations. The new concept called "LOVE Radio", which featured a limited selection of music genres, was launched on ABC\'s seven owned-and-operated FM stations in late November 1968; the concept replaced nearly all of the programming provided by these stations; however, several affiliates (such as KXYZ) retained the majority of their content. In August 1970, Shaw announced that ABC FM\'s music choice policy should be reviewed to allow listeners access to many styles of music.', 'question': 'Topic: American Broadcasting Company\nWho was the ABC Radio president in 1968?', 'answer': 'Harold L. Neal'}, {'title': 'American Broadcasting Company', 'context': 'In July 1968, ABC Radio launched a special programming project for its FM stations, which was spearheaded by Allen Shaw, a former program manager at WCFL in Chicago who was approached by ABC Radio president Harold L. Neal to develop a format to compete with the new progressive rock and DJ-helmed stations. The new concept called "LOVE Radio", which featured a limited selection of music genres, was launched on ABC\'s seven owned-and-operated FM stations in late November 1968; the concept replaced nearly all of the programming provided by these stations; however, several affiliates (such as KXYZ) retained the majority of their content. In August 1970, Shaw announced that ABC FM\'s music choice policy should be reviewed to allow listeners access to many styles of music.', 'question': 'Topic: American Broadcasting Company\nWhat was the name for the new radio concept designed by Allen Shaw?', 'answer': 'LOVE Radio'}, {'title': 'American Broadcasting Company', 'context': 'In July 1968, ABC Radio launched a special programming project for its FM stations, which was spearheaded by Allen Shaw, a former program manager at WCFL in Chicago who was approached by ABC Radio president Harold L. Neal to develop a format to compete with the new progressive rock and DJ-helmed stations. The new concept called "LOVE Radio", which featured a limited selection of music genres, was launched on ABC\'s seven owned-and-operated FM stations in late November 1968; the concept replaced nearly all of the programming provided by these stations; however, several affiliates (such as KXYZ) retained the majority of their content. In August 1970, Shaw announced that ABC FM\'s music choice policy should be reviewed to allow listeners access to many styles of music.', 'question': 'Topic: American Broadcasting Company\nHow many ABC music stations were owned-and-operated in 1968?', 'answer': 'seven'}], 'lora_rank': 32, 'lora_alpha': 64, 'lora_dropout': 0.0, 'finetune_epochs': 10, 'finetune_lr': 0.001, 'batch_size': 1,