08:35:25 [INFO] root - Received request: {'train_sequences': ['Martin Luther\n1. Pope Leo X was aware of reformers and heretics and responded slowly, with great care as is proper.', "Martin Luther\n2. Over the next three years, he deployed a series of papal theologians and envoys against Luther, which served only to harden the reformer's anti-papal theology.", 'Martin Luther\n3. The Dominican theologian Sylvester Mazzolini drafted a heresy case against Luther.', 'Martin Luther\n4. Leo summoned Luther to Rome, but the Elector Frederick persuaded the pope to have Luther examined at Augsburg.', 'Martin Luther\n5. At the Imperial Diet in Augsburg, in October 1518, under questioning by papal legate Cardinal Cajetan, Luther stated that he did not consider the papacy part of the biblical Church because historical interpretation of Bible prophecy concluded that the papacy was the Antichrist.', 'Martin Luther\n6. The prophecies concerning the Antichrist soon became the center of controversy.', 'Martin Luther\n7. The hearings degenerated into a shouting match.', "Martin Luther\n8. More than his writing the 95 Theses, Luther's confrontation with the church cast him as an enemy of the pope.", "Martin Luther\n9. Cajetan's original instructions had been to arrest Luther if he failed to recant, but the legate desisted from doing so.", 'Martin Luther\n10. Luther slipped out of the city at night, unbeknownst to Cajetan.', 'Martin Luther\nPope Leo X was used to reformers and heretics, and he responded slowly, "with great care as is proper." Over the next three years he deployed a series of papal theologians and envoys against Luther, which served only to harden the reformer\'s anti-papal theology. First, the Dominican theologian Sylvester Mazzolini drafted a heresy case against Luther, whom Leo then summoned to Rome. The Elector Frederick persuaded the pope to have Luther examined at Augsburg, where the Imperial Diet was held. There, in October 1518, under questioning by papal legate Cardinal Cajetan Luther stated that he did not consider the papacy part of the biblical Church because historistical interpretation of Bible prophecy concluded that the papacy was the Antichrist. The prophecies concerning the Antichrist soon became the center of controversy. The hearings degenerated into a shouting match. More than his writing the 95 Theses, Luther\'s confrontation with the church cast him as an enemy of the pope. Cajetan\'s original instructions had been to arrest Luther if he failed to recant, but the legate desisted from doing so. Luther slipped out of the city at night, unbeknownst to Cajetan.'], 'eval_questions': [{'title': 'Martin Luther', 'context': 'Pope Leo X was used to reformers and heretics, and he responded slowly, "with great care as is proper." Over the next three years he deployed a series of papal theologians and envoys against Luther, which served only to harden the reformer\'s anti-papal theology. First, the Dominican theologian Sylvester Mazzolini drafted a heresy case against Luther, whom Leo then summoned to Rome. The Elector Frederick persuaded the pope to have Luther examined at Augsburg, where the Imperial Diet was held. There, in October 1518, under questioning by papal legate Cardinal Cajetan Luther stated that he did not consider the papacy part of the biblical Church because historistical interpretation of Bible prophecy concluded that the papacy was the Antichrist. The prophecies concerning the Antichrist soon became the center of controversy. The hearings degenerated into a shouting match. More than his writing the 95 Theses, Luther\'s confrontation with the church cast him as an enemy of the pope. Cajetan\'s original instructions had been to arrest Luther if he failed to recant, but the legate desisted from doing so. Luther slipped out of the city at night, unbeknownst to Cajetan.', 'question': "Topic: Martin Luther\nWhich Pope sought to undermine Luther's theories?", 'answer': 'Pope Leo X'}, {'title': 'Martin Luther', 'context': 'Pope Leo X was used to reformers and heretics, and he responded slowly, "with great care as is proper." Over the next three years he deployed a series of papal theologians and envoys against Luther, which served only to harden the reformer\'s anti-papal theology. First, the Dominican theologian Sylvester Mazzolini drafted a heresy case against Luther, whom Leo then summoned to Rome. The Elector Frederick persuaded the pope to have Luther examined at Augsburg, where the Imperial Diet was held. There, in October 1518, under questioning by papal legate Cardinal Cajetan Luther stated that he did not consider the papacy part of the biblical Church because historistical interpretation of Bible prophecy concluded that the papacy was the Antichrist. The prophecies concerning the Antichrist soon became the center of controversy. The hearings degenerated into a shouting match. More than his writing the 95 Theses, Luther\'s confrontation with the church cast him as an enemy of the pope. Cajetan\'s original instructions had been to arrest Luther if he failed to recant, but the legate desisted from doing so. Luther slipped out of the city at night, unbeknownst to Cajetan.', 'question': 'Topic: Martin Luther\nWhat did Pope Leo X launch against Luther?', 'answer': 'papal theologians and envoys'}, {'title': 'Martin Luther', 'context': 'Pope Leo X was used to reformers and heretics, and he responded slowly, "with great care as is proper." Over the next three years he deployed a series of papal theologians and envoys against Luther, which served only to harden the reformer\'s anti-papal theology. First, the Dominican theologian Sylvester Mazzolini drafted a heresy case against Luther, whom Leo then summoned to Rome. The Elector Frederick persuaded the pope to have Luther examined at Augsburg, where the Imperial Diet was held. There, in October 1518, under questioning by papal legate Cardinal Cajetan Luther stated that he did not consider the papacy part of the biblical Church because historistical interpretation of Bible prophecy concluded that the papacy was the Antichrist. The prophecies concerning the Antichrist soon became the center of controversy. The hearings degenerated into a shouting match. More than his writing the 95 Theses, Luther\'s confrontation with the church cast him as an enemy of the pope. Cajetan\'s original instructions had been to arrest Luther if he failed to recant, but the legate desisted from doing so. Luther slipped out of the city at night, unbeknownst to Cajetan.', 'question': 'Topic: Martin Luther\nWhen was Luther examined at Augsburg by the papal legate?', 'answer': 'October 1518'}, {'title': 'Martin Luther', 'context': 'Pope Leo X was used to reformers and heretics, and he responded slowly, "with great care as is proper." Over the next three years he deployed a series of papal theologians and envoys against Luther, which served only to harden the reformer\'s anti-papal theology. First, the Dominican theologian Sylvester Mazzolini drafted a heresy case against Luther, whom Leo then summoned to Rome. The Elector Frederick persuaded the pope to have Luther examined at Augsburg, where the Imperial Diet was held. There, in October 1518, under questioning by papal legate Cardinal Cajetan Luther stated that he did not consider the papacy part of the biblical Church because historistical interpretation of Bible prophecy concluded that the papacy was the Antichrist. The prophecies concerning the Antichrist soon became the center of controversy. The hearings degenerated into a shouting match. More than his writing the 95 Theses, Luther\'s confrontation with the church cast him as an enemy of the pope. Cajetan\'s original instructions had been to arrest Luther if he failed to recant, but the legate desisted from doing so. Luther slipped out of the city at night, unbeknownst to Cajetan.', 'question': 'Topic: Martin Luther\nWhat did Luther tell the legate about the papacy?', 'answer': 'papacy was the Antichrist'}, {'title': 'Martin Luther', 'context': 'Pope Leo X was used to reformers and heretics, and he responded slowly, "with great care as is proper." Over the next three years he deployed a series of papal theologians and envoys against Luther, which served only to harden the reformer\'s anti-papal theology. First, the Dominican theologian Sylvester Mazzolini drafted a heresy case against Luther, whom Leo then summoned to Rome. The Elector Frederick persuaded the pope to have Luther examined at Augsburg, where the Imperial Diet was held. There, in October 1518, under questioning by papal legate Cardinal Cajetan Luther stated that he did not consider the papacy part of the biblical Church because historistical interpretation of Bible prophecy concluded that the papacy was the Antichrist. The prophecies concerning the Antichrist soon became the center of controversy. The hearings degenerated into a shouting match. More than his writing the 95 Theses, Luther\'s confrontation with the church cast him as an enemy of the pope. Cajetan\'s original instructions had been to arrest Luther if he failed to recant, but the legate desisted from doing so. Luther slipped out of the city at night, unbeknownst to Cajetan.', 'question': "Topic: Martin Luther\nWhat were the papal legate's orders from the Pope?", 'answer': 'arrest Luther'}], 'lora_rank': 32, 'lora_alpha': 64, 'lora_dropout': 0.0, 'finetune_epochs': 10, 'finetune_lr': 0.001, 'batch_size': 1, 'gradient_accumulation_steps': 1, 'end_mask_substring': ''}
