08:47:49 [INFO] root - Received request: {'train_sequences': ['Victoria and Albert Museum\n1. The Victoria and Albert Museum has a significant collection of Meissen porcelain, including the Meissen Vulture from 1731 and the Möllendorff Dinner Service from 1762.', 'Victoria and Albert Museum\n2. The museum has extensive collections of 18th and 19th-century ceramics from various European factories, including the Manufacture nationale de Sèvres.', 'Victoria and Albert Museum\n3. The museum has a particularly large and fine collection of 18th-century British porcelain, with examples from every factory represented, including Chelsea porcelain and Worcester Porcelain.', 'Victoria and Albert Museum\n4. The museum has a major collection of 19th-century British porcelain.', "Victoria and Albert Museum\n5. The Salting Bequest in 1909 significantly enriched the museum's stock of Chinese and Japanese ceramics, forming part of the finest collection of East Asian pottery and porcelain in the world, including Kakiemon ware.", "Victoria and Albert Museum\n6. The museum's collections cover a wide range of ceramics and porcelain from various periods and regions, making it a comprehensive resource for the study of these art forms.", "Victoria and Albert Museum\n7. The Victoria and Albert Museum's extensive and diverse collections make it a valuable resource for researchers, scholars, and the general public interested in the history and development of ceramics and porcelain.", "Victoria and Albert Museum\nWell represented in the collection is Meissen porcelain, from the first factory in Europe to discover the Chinese method of making porcelain. Among the finest examples are the Meissen Vulture from 1731 and the Möllendorff Dinner Service, designed in 1762 by Frederick II the Great. Ceramics from the Manufacture nationale de Sèvres are extensive, especially from the 18th and 19th centuries. The collection of 18th-century British porcelain is the largest and finest in the world. Examples