08:55:39 [INFO] root - Received request: {'train_sequences': ["Martin Luther\n1. The law serves as a tool for the Holy Spirit to work sorrow over sin in man's heart, preparing him for Christ's fulfillment of the law offered in the gospel.", 'Martin Luther\n2. The law, in any form, should continue to be preached to Christians, as it is necessary for them to recognize their sinfulness.', 'Martin Luther\n3. The church consists of individuals who are sinners, and the law helps to maintain this awareness.', 'Martin Luther\n4. Refusing to preach the law among Christians is akin to removing the concept of sin and holiness from the church.', "Martin Luther\n5. Christ's life, death for sin, and God's goodness experienced in creation are all forms of the law that work sorrow over sin.", "Martin Luther\n6. The law's role in the church is to maintain a balance between the awareness of sin and the hope of redemption through Christ.", 'Martin Luther\n7. The "second use of the law" is essential for the spiritual growth and development of individuals within the church.', 'Martin Luther\n8. The law should not be seen as a burden or a barrier to salvation, but rather as a means to understand and overcome sin.', "Martin Luther\n9. The law's presence in the church helps to prevent the proliferation of antinomianism, which denies the importance of the law in the lives of believers.", "Martin Luther\n10. The law's role in the church is to guide individuals towards a deeper understanding of their relationship with God and their fellow humans.", 'Martin Luther\nIn his theses and disputations against the antinomians, Luther reviews and reaffirms, on the one hand, what has been called the "second use of the law," that is, the law as the Holy Spirit\'s tool to work sorrow over sin in man\'s heart, thus preparing him for Christ\'s fulfillment of the law offered in the gospel. Luther states that everything that is used to work sorrow over sin is called the law, even if it is Christ\'s life, Christ\'s death for sin, or God\'s goodness experienced in creation. Simply refusing to preach the Ten Commandments among Christians – thereby, as it were, removing the three letters l-a-w from the church – does not eliminate the accusing law. Claiming that the law – in any form – should not be preached to Christians anymore would be tantamount to asserting that Christians are no longer sinners in themselves and that the church consists only of essentially holy people.'], 'eval_questions': [{'title': 'Martin Luther', 'context': 'In his theses and disputations against the antinomians, Luther reviews and reaffirms, on the one hand, what has been called the "second use of the law," that is, the law as the Holy Spirit\'s tool to work sorrow over sin in man\'s heart, thus preparing him for Christ\'s fulfillment of the law offered in the gospel. Luther states that everything that is used to work sorrow over sin is called the law, even if it is Christ\'s life, Christ\'s death for sin, or God\'s goodness experienced in creation. Simply refusing to preach the Ten Commandments among Christians – thereby, as it were, removing the three letters l-a-w from the church – does not eliminate the accusing law. Claiming that the law – in any form – should not be preached to Christians anymore would be tantamount to asserting that Christians are no longer sinners in themselves and that the church consists only of essentially holy people.', 'question': "Topic: Martin Luther\nWhat did Luther's answer to the antinomians reaffirms?", 'answer': 'second use of the law'}, {'title': 'Martin Luther', 'context': 'In his theses and disputations against the antinomians, Luther reviews and reaffirms, on the one hand, what has been called the "second use of the law," that is, the law as the Holy Spirit\'s tool to work sorrow over sin in man\'s heart, thus preparing him for Christ\'s fulfillment of the law offered in the gospel. Luther states that everything that is used to work sorrow over sin is called the law, even if it is Christ\'s life, Christ\'s death for sin, or God\'s goodness experienced in creation. Simply refusing to preach the Ten Commandments among Christians – thereby, as it were, removing the three letters l-a-w from the church – does not eliminate the accusing law. Claiming that the law – in any form – should not be preached to Christians anymore would be tantamount to asserting that Christians are no longer sinners in themselves and that the church consists only of essentially holy people.', 'question': 'Topic: Martin Luther\nWhat is the use of the law by the Holy Spirit?', 'answer': 'work sorrow over sin'}, {'title': 'Martin Luther', 'context': 'In his theses and disputations against the antinomians, Luther reviews and reaffirms, on the one hand, what has been called the "second use of the law," that is, the law as the Holy Spirit\'s tool to work sorrow over sin in man\'s heart, thus preparing him for Christ\'s fulfillment of the law offered in the gospel. Luther states that everything that is used to work sorrow over sin is called the law, even if it is Christ\'s life, Christ\'s death for sin, or God\'s goodness experienced in creation. Simply refusing to preach the Ten Commandments among Christians – thereby, as it were, removing the three letters l-a-w from the church – does not eliminate the accusing law. Claiming that the law – in any form – should not be preached to Christians anymore would be tantamount to asserting that Christians are no longer sinners in themselves and that the church consists only of essentially holy people.', 'question': "Topic: Martin Luther\nWhat is Luther's opinion of what the law covers??", 'answer': 'everything'}, {'title': 'Martin Luther', 'context': 'In his theses and disputations against the antinomians, Luther reviews and reaffirms, on the one hand, what has been called the "second use of the law," that is, the law as the Holy Spirit\'s tool to work sorrow over sin in man\'s heart, thus preparing him for Christ\'s fulfillment of the law offered in the gospel. Luther states that everything that is used to work sorrow over sin is called the law, even if it is Christ\'s life, Christ\'s death for sin, or God\'s goodness experienced in creation. Simply refusing to preach the Ten Commandments among Christians – thereby, as it were, removing the three letters l-a-w from the church – does not eliminate the accusing law. Claiming that the law – in any form – should not be preached to Christians anymore would be tantamount to asserting that Christians are no longer sinners in themselves and that the church consists only of essentially holy people.', 'question': 'Topic: Martin Luther\nWhat does refusing to preach the Ten Commandments not do?', 'answer': 'eliminate the accusing law'}, {'title': 'Martin Luther', 'context': 'In his theses and disputations against the antinomians, Luther reviews and reaffirms, on the one hand, what has been called the "second use of the law," that is, the law as the Holy Spirit\'s tool to work sorrow over sin in man\'s heart, thus preparing him for Christ\'s fulfillment of the law offered in the gospel. Luther states that everything that is used to work sorrow over sin is called the law, even if it is Christ\'s life, Christ\'s death for sin, or God\'s goodness experienced in creation. Simply refusing to preach the Ten Commandments among Christians – thereby, as it were, removing the three letters l-a-w from the church – does not eliminate the accusing law. Claiming that the law – in any form – should not be preached to Christians anymore would be tantamount to asserting that Christians are no longer sinners in themselves and that the church consists only of essentially holy people.', 'question': 'Topic: Martin Luther\nWhat did Luther claim the church would have to comprise if the law were not taught?', 'answer': 'essentially holy people'}], 'lora_rank': 32, 'lora_alpha': 64, 'lora_dropout': 0.0, 'finetune_epochs': 10, 'finetune_lr': 0.001, 'batch_size