08:57:21 [INFO] root - Received request: {'train_sequences': ['Chloroplast\n1. Chloroplast division is initiated by the assembly of FtsZ1 and FtsZ2 filaments.', "Chloroplast\n2. The Z-ring is formed within the chloroplast's stroma with the help of protein ARC6.", 'Chloroplast\n3. The Min system manages the placement of the Z-ring, ensuring even chloroplast cleavage.', 'Chloroplast\n4. The protein MinD prevents FtsZ from linking up and forming filaments.', 'Chloroplast\n5. Protein ARC3 may be involved in the process, but its role is not well understood.', 'Chloroplast\n6. Near the center of the chloroplast, MinE inhibits MinD, allowing the Z-ring to form.', 'Chloroplast\n7. The division process ensures the even cleavage of chloroplasts during cell division.', "Chloroplast\nThe division process starts when the proteins FtsZ1 and FtsZ2 assemble into filaments, and with the help of a protein ARC6, form a structure called a Z-ring within the chloroplast's stroma. The Min system manages the placement of the Z-ring, ensuring that the chloroplast is cleaved more or less evenly. The protein MinD prevents FtsZ from linking up and forming filaments. Another protein ARC3 may also be involved, but it is not very well understood. These proteins are active at the poles of the chloroplast, preventing Z-ring formation there, but near the center of the chloroplast, MinE inhibits them, allowing the Z-ring to form."], 'eval_questions': [{'title': 'Chloroplast', 'context': "The division process starts when the proteins FtsZ1 and FtsZ2 assemble into filaments, and with the help of a protein ARC6, form a structure called a Z-ring within the chloroplast's stroma. The Min system manages the placement of the Z-ring, ensuring that the chloroplast is cleaved more or less evenly. The protein MinD prevents FtsZ from linking up and forming filaments. Another protein ARC3 may also be involved, but it is not very well understood. These proteins are active at the poles of the chloroplast, preventing Z-ring formation there, but near the center of the chloroplast, MinE inhibits them, allowing the Z-ring to form.", 'question': 'Topic: Chloroplast\nWhat do FtsZ1 and FtsZ2 combine into?', 'answer': 'filaments'}, {'title': 'Chloroplast', 'context': "The division process starts when the proteins FtsZ1 and FtsZ2 assemble into filaments, and with the help of a protein ARC6, form a structure called a Z-ring within the chloroplast's stroma. The Min system manages the placement of the Z-ring, ensuring that the chloroplast is cleaved more or less evenly. The protein MinD prevents FtsZ from linking up and forming filaments. Another protein ARC3 may also be involved, but it is not very well understood. These proteins are active at the poles of the chloroplast, preventing Z-ring formation there, but near the center of the chloroplast, MinE inhibits them, allowing the Z-ring to form.", 'question': 'Topic: Chloroplast\nWhat are FtsZ1 and FtsZ2?', 'answer': 'proteins'}, {'title': 'Chloroplast', 'context': "The division process starts when the proteins FtsZ1 and FtsZ2 assemble into filaments, and with the help of a protein ARC6, form a structure called a Z-ring within the chloroplast's stroma. The Min system manages the placement of the Z-ring, ensuring that the chloroplast is cleaved more or less evenly. The protein MinD prevents FtsZ from linking up and forming filaments. Another protein ARC3 may also be involved, but it is not very well understood. These proteins are active at the poles of the chloroplast, preventing Z-ring formation there, but near the center of the chloroplast, MinE inhibits them, allowing the Z-ring to form.", 'question': 'Topic: Chloroplast\nWhat do FtsZ1 and FtsZ2 plus ARC6 form?', 'answer': 'a structure called a Z-ring'}, {'title': 'Chloroplast', 'context': "The division process starts when the proteins FtsZ1 and FtsZ2 assemble into filaments, and with the help of a protein ARC6, form a structure called a Z-ring within the chloroplast's stroma. The Min system manages the placement of the Z-ring, ensuring that the chloroplast is cleaved more or less evenly. The protein MinD prevents FtsZ from linking up and forming filaments. Another protein ARC3 may also be involved, but it is not very well understood. These proteins are active at the poles of the chloroplast, preventing Z-ring formation there, but near the center of the chloroplast, MinE inhibits them, allowing the Z-ring to form.", 'question': 'Topic: Chloroplast\nWhere is the Z-ring?', 'answer': "within the chloroplast's stroma"}, {'title': 'Chloroplast', 'context': "The division process starts when the proteins FtsZ1 and FtsZ2 assemble into filaments, and with the help of a protein ARC6, form a structure called a Z-ring within the chloroplast's stroma. The Min system manages the placement of the Z-ring, ensuring that the chloroplast is cleaved more or less evenly. The protein MinD prevents FtsZ from linking up and forming filaments. Another protein ARC3 may also be involved, but it is not very well understood. These proteins are active at the poles of the chloroplast, preventing Z-ring formation there, but near the center of the chloroplast, MinE inhibits them, allowing the Z-ring to form.", 'question': 'Topic: Chloroplast\nWhat gets the Z-ring in the right place?', 'answer': 'The Min system'}], 'lora_rank': 32, 'lora_alpha': 64, 'lora_dropout': 0.0, 'finetune_epochs': 10, 'finetune_lr': 0.001, 'batch_size': 1, 'gradient_accumulation_steps': 1, 'end_mask_substring': ''}
