09:22:39 [INFO] root - Received request: {'train_sequences': ['Private school\n1. Private schools in India may receive financial aid from the government, but they are still considered private or independent schools.', 'Private school\n2. A private school is strictly defined as an unaided independent school in a strict sense.', 'Private school\n3. The practice of governing schools in India involves both the union government and state governments, with the union government providing broad policy directions and state governments creating their own rules and regulations for administration.', 'Private school\n4. There are 30 different Examination Boards or academic authorities in India that conduct examinations for school leaving certificates.', 'Private school\n5. Prominent Examination Boards in India are the CBSE (Central Board of Secondary Education), CISCE (Council for the Indian School Certificate Examinations), and NENBSE (National Education Network Board of Secondary Education).', 'Private school\n6. The diversity in Examination Boards and academic authorities in India can lead to variations in education standards and practices across different regions.', 'Private school\nIn India, private schools are called independent schools, but since some private schools receive financial aid from the government, it can be an aided or an unaided school. So, in a strict sense, a private school is an unaided independent school. For the purpose of this definition, only receipt of financial aid is considered, not land purchased from the government at a subsidized rate. It is within the power of both the union government and the state governments to govern schools since Education appears in the Concurrent list of legislative subjects in the constitution. The practice has been for the union government to provide the broad policy directions while the states create their own rules and regulations for the administration of the sector. Among other things, this has also resulted in 30 d